WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Film Courage: Does the film industry make&nbsp;
people crazy? And if not, why that perception?

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.840
Shane Stanley, Filmmaker/Author/Instructor:&nbsp;
Yes, I think there’s a lot of crazy that comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.760
hand-in-hand with being an artist&nbsp;
anyway. There is eccentricity with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:20.880
creative beings, we’re dreamers. I think any&nbsp;
time you live your life on kind of playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.720
the odds of the lottery, how does it not make&nbsp;
you crazy. Because I talk about it in my book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:33.600
what other business do you know of really…music,&nbsp;
film, dance, art, where you could literally be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.960
homeless living on a friend’s couch and get&nbsp;
an audition for that one role and become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:46.800
the next Gal Gadot, you can become the next&nbsp;
Jason Bateman, you can be anybody. And you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.040
know where you think about doctors, lawyers,&nbsp;
successful business people, they have a path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.200
they still might be able to figure out&nbsp;
that path, but they usually go to college.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:03.760
They will often figure out a plan and a road, get&nbsp;
their eye on a prize and work their way to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.320
Medical school being six years and then&nbsp;
residency, that’s a game plan. For artists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.160
the game plan is what we do is bust your behind to&nbsp;
make it. You can fake it until you make it, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:22.160
never give up, it’s I can’t quit because my break&nbsp;
may come tomorrow and I think a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:31.040
coming up that way and trying find their path&nbsp;
(their place) it can make you crazy. I’ve found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.600
trying to find my path has made me a little nuts&nbsp;
sometimes as I say a little south of sanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.760
I also find once we get into a position of&nbsp;
authority I think no other industry is as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.920
unkind as our’s. And I know everybody says&nbsp;
there’s horrible people in every industry. I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:58.000
been around every industry you can imagine because&nbsp;
of what I do and the things that I’ve experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:05.680
and shot and it’s mind-boggling how people are&nbsp;
treated and yes I think the me too movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.960
and then the aftermath of that and people and&nbsp;
how we talk to one another was so long overdue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.320
but it’s amazing what people can get away&nbsp;
with and I still think they get away with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:22.720
and how they talk to each other or treat people&nbsp;
and that can make people crazy. I think it does.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:30.720
Film Courage: Sure. What about people’s reactions&nbsp;
to artists? There are all of these stigmas that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.320
artists are selfish or whatever. Can&nbsp;
we talk about other people’s reactions?

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:36.560
Shane: Can I swear?

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.760
Film Courage: Uh…okay. One swear word.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:46.480
Shane: Okay one bad word. I have a brother that&nbsp;
I love more than anything. He’s very successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.200
and he has a motto that he’s always kind&nbsp;
of looked at what we do and he’s always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.200
said look you want to be an artist? Go paint&nbsp;
in the freakin park on Saturday. Get a job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:02.960
life is a business…don’t…don’t…life goes by fast.&nbsp;
You don’t want to look back when you are 40 or 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.720
and wonder what happened. He is the smart squirrel&nbsp;
that always stashed away during the winter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:17.520
(for the winter months) and I see that. My&nbsp;
mother was from Manhattan (from New York).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:23.920
A very successful lawyer was her father. She was a&nbsp;
very successful real estate agent. Her brother was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.280
the director of primary care and the fact that&nbsp;
I didn’t go to college and the fact that I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.240
wanted to be a dreamer, figure out what I’m going&nbsp;
to want to do with myself and the three choices I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.920
picked were music, dirt bikes and films. You&nbsp;
don’t need a college education for either and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:46.400
all of them are pretty big shots in the dark.&nbsp;
I think people look at artists and as I call us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:55.680
people who express themselves for a living,&nbsp;
a lot of people don’t understand it. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.960
find when I talk with different schools&nbsp;
(different institutions), a lot of parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.960
are more supportive now than when I was growing&nbsp;
up because you have things like you know somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:12.880
like Mark Zuckerberg coming along and creating&nbsp;
Facebook. You have somebody who created Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:19.440
or Pinterest. All these things that one day they&nbsp;
wrote a code for something and that made them a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:26.080
billionaire overnight. So I think we have a little&nbsp;
more leeway and a little more grace from people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:33.200
who look down at the dreamers and the creative&nbsp;
beings. But I still get sad when I talk to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.760
lot of the youngsters coming up and they don’t&nbsp;
have the support from their family especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.600
the ones who are going to community college&nbsp;
because they don’t have the financial means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:46.080
to put themselves through a good school and they&nbsp;
certainly don’t have the support of parents. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.520
actually encountered some very well-to-do in the&nbsp;
community colleges whose parents are like I’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.840
writ the check to go to USC tomorrow. You want&nbsp;
to to go to Yale? We will make it happen but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.360
you’re going to go down this path, you’re on your&nbsp;
own. And I think that’s unfortunate. So I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:08.000
there’s a little bit of a stigma still but I don’t&nbsp;
think it’s as bad as it was when I was coming up.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.080
Film Courage: Do you think people in community&nbsp;
colleges are less competitive (I’m going off on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.280
a little bit of a tangent) with one another? I&nbsp;
mean I went to a community college and at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.360
time the internet was just starting up&nbsp;
so you couldn’t look somebody up and say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.800
“What’s this person’s deal?” So you&nbsp;
could be more anonymous. I felt it wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.240
uber competitive and I was&nbsp;
able to enjoy myself there.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.640
Shane: I’ve gotten to the point where&nbsp;
whenever a community college says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:39.440
“would you…could you…can you…” The answer is&nbsp;
“yes,” because there’s a purity, there’s a drive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:44.400
there’s a determination without prejudice. I find&nbsp;
a lot of people at community colleges now are on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:51.040
their second wave of life. They are often parents,&nbsp;
they take buses and public modes of transportation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.200
to get to school and they are putting themselves&nbsp;
through and paying a few hundred dollars a unit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:04.400
to try to get to something and I enjoy that crowd&nbsp;
a lot. I do work with a lot of universities and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:11.760
appreciate and cherish that time too but the&nbsp;
mindset is different. I can tell you that (without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.960
naming names) there are universities that accept&nbsp;
the fact that their students will not finish their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:22.720
thesis film after having three years to do it and&nbsp;
they still get a beautiful diploma and march down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:32.160
the stage and I don’t understand that. Our&nbsp;
business will not hire you if you don’t finish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.520
a task. If you don’t show up and you don’t&nbsp;
deliver, you don’t work. So I don’t know why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:43.200
you’d reward them and I had a chair at a very&nbsp;
prominent film school university tell me once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:48.640
we don’t want to deal with the lawsuits, so we&nbsp;
pass them and if I’m breaking protocol by coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:53.920
out and saying that I don’t care. That’s how it&nbsp;
is and that doesn’t happen at community college.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:07:01.200
You have guys like Dan Watanabe and Chris Rossiter&nbsp;
and some wonderful teachers who have been that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.240
done that and spent more time on the set than&nbsp;
most university professors have and they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.920
very happy to fail you if you don’t do the work&nbsp;
and I appreciate that because that’s how it.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:17.105
Film Courage: Remember Alex P. Keaton from&nbsp;
Family Ties [played by Michael J. Fox]?

00:07:17.105 --> 00:07:18.464
Shane: Love him! Marty McFly!

00:07:18.464 --> 00:07:23.840
Film Courage: He was always throwing community&nbsp;
colleges under the bus. That was his go to thing.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:32.320
Shane: Hey I think they are salt of the earth&nbsp;
and it’s funny I will tell you when I first wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:33.440
my book [WHAT YOU DON’T LEARN IN FILM SCHOOL: A&nbsp;
Complete Guide To (Independent) Filmmaking] and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:36.560
a few of the universities were getting&nbsp;
excited and a few blacklisted me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.080
just by the title of the book because I used&nbsp;
to be welcome to give lectures and workshops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:46.320
and speak and then as soon as they read the&nbsp;
manuscript it was Oh well the title of your book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:51.040
you’re out. It’s like I have no problem with film&nbsp;
school. If you didn’t grow up around it, you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:54.640
to learn these things. All I want to do is teach&nbsp;
you the things they don’t teach you. There is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:00.960
business of the business of distribution, how to&nbsp;
get financing. You know not French New Wave or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.960
what angle to put the camera and the lights at.&nbsp;
There is so much more to learn, doing your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.520
deals, production deals, distribution agreements,&nbsp;
actor agreements, how to approach talent, how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:15.600
approach money. You don’t talk about that in film&nbsp;
school. That’s what I teach. And when I first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:21.520
got people excited about the book and well there’s&nbsp;
these nine community colleges where you are and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:26.960
we’d really like to get you roped in with them&nbsp;
I was first a little bit like what am I getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:32.480
into here? I went and spoke for the first time&nbsp;
at a community college and I never looked back. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:39.040
love the passion these people had and they are&nbsp;
not kids. We have people our age there. I go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:42.960
to these workshops, there are people in their&nbsp;
60’s and 70’s trying to figure out their next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.440
chapter and I love that because they’ve been that,&nbsp;
done that and they’re old souls that have lived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.880
They’ve scrapped for everything they’ve had,&nbsp;
they’ve fought for every piece of real estate they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.880 --> 00:09:07.840
own usually and I just think that makes for better&nbsp;
artists. I think it makes for better human beings.

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:19.680
Question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.994
for the Viewers: Does the film&nbsp;
industry make people crazy?

